{"t": "2026-10-14T14:05:22", "stage": "C3", "verb": "log", "args": ["Explanation shelf, read whole (verified): Puranam, Alexy and Reitzig (2014). Its claims that bear here: a form of organizing is a set of solutions to four universal problems, individually necessary and collectively sufficient (p. 166); goalless networks and crowds are ruled out (p. 164); self-selection, transparent task division, intrinsic reward and virtual collaboration are complements (pp. 173-174); visible work simplifies coordination (p. 172); authority in open source is the right to accept or reject contributions and membership (p. 173). First reading: it is the standing claim for C3c, and it resembles more than it contradicts (visible work, a host's right to accept guests). Next: Felin and Zenger (2017)."], "opts": {"kind": "note"}, "offset": 0, "limit": 80, "lines": 1, "total": 1, "refused": null}
